# gstack is the default workflow

I'm a marketer, not a developer. gstack is my agent orchestration layer — a set of skills that act like a team of specialists (CEO, eng manager, designer, QA lead, security officer, release engineer). Every skill is prefixed with `gstack-` so there's no ambiguity with built-in commands.

## Reach for gstack first

Match the user's intent to the right gstack skill. Proactively suggest it when the situation fits, unless told to stop suggesting.

| User intent | gstack skill |
|---|---|
| New feature or product idea | `/gstack-office-hours` (reframe it), then `/gstack-autoplan` |
| Before writing any non-trivial code | `/gstack-autoplan` (runs CEO → design → eng review) |
| After writing code, before shipping | `/gstack-review` (auto-fixes issues) |
| QA test a URL | `/gstack-qa <url>` (real browser, finds + fixes bugs) |
| Shipping | `/gstack-ship` (tests + PR) then `/gstack-land-and-deploy` |
| Can't figure out a bug | `/gstack-investigate` (root-cause methodology) |
| Security audit before launch | `/gstack-cso` (OWASP + STRIDE) |
| Design work | `/gstack-design-consultation`, `/gstack-design-shotgun`, `/gstack-design-html` |
| Performance baseline | `/gstack-benchmark` |
| Monitor after deploy | `/gstack-canary` |

## Browser work

For any web browsing, QA, screenshots, or scraping, use `/gstack-browse` (not `mcp__claude-in-chrome__*` tools — those are slower and not what I use).

## Safety

- Before destructive commands (rm -rf, force-push, DROP TABLE, git reset --hard), warn me and suggest `/gstack-guard`.
- Never skip git hooks (`--no-verify`) without asking.
- Confirm before pushing, merging, or anything that affects shared state.

## When I'm stuck

I don't code. If you hit a decision that needs judgment I can't make (architecture, naming, tradeoffs), ask me in plain language what outcome I want — not which implementation to pick. Then make the call yourself.
